+# Build loading and unloading
+
+## The problem
+
+When a player with a 20,000-block save joined, `Builds.server.luau` built the whole save in one
+server frame. Each block was three instances: the Part, an `Owner` StringValue and a `PlacedAt`
+NumberValue. The Part was also parented (`Instance.new("Part", parent)`) before its properties
+were set. That made 60,000 instances, plus a property change on each of them after it was already
+in the DataModel, all in one call to `onPlayerAdded`. The MicroProfiler showed this as a
+~2,100 ms frame under `Script_Builds`, and every player rubber-banded.
+
+All 60,000 creations then reached every client in one burst, which caused the
+`Replicator ProcessPackets` hitch on clients.
+
+Leaving did the same thing in reverse. One frame called `GetChildren()` on 20,000 parts, read
+`Position` on each one, and destroyed 60,000 instances.
+
+## What changed
+
+All of this is in `src/ServerScriptService/Builds.server.luau`.
+
+1. **One instance per block.** Owner, placed time and grid cell now live in a server-only table,
+   `blockInfo[part] = { plot, ownerId, placedAt, cell }`, so each block has no child value
+   objects. That's a third of the instances to create, replicate and destroy. Placed time is
+   still kept for every block, both loaded and newly placed, with the same value as before
+   (`SavedBlock.PlacedAt`, or `os.time()` when placed).
+2. **Parts are cloned from templates.** There's one unparented, fully set-up Part per block type.
+   Each block is `template:Clone()`, then its `CFrame` is set, then it's parented. No property
+   changes happen after the part is in the world.
+3. **Loading is spread over frames.** `onPlayerAdded` only takes a plot slot, creates the folder,
+   calls `SavedBuilds.load` and queues the plot. A `Heartbeat` worker then does the work in two
+   phases:
+   - **Reserve:** claim the save's cells in `occupied`. This is pure table work (up to 5,000
+     cells per frame), so a full save finishes in about 4 frames.
+   - **Create:** make the parts, at most **200 per frame**.
+
+   The worker stops each frame once it has spent **3 ms**, and the budget is shared by every
+   plot. Eight players joining together cost no more per frame than one; their builds just queue.
+4. **Unloading is spread over frames too.** When a player leaves, the plot goes on an unload queue
+   that runs before loads. It destroys up to **1,000 blocks per frame** within the same 3 ms
+   budget and frees each cell as its block goes. Cells that were reserved but never built (the
+   player left mid-load) are released right away.
+5. **A slot stays taken until its plot has fully unloaded.** That way a new build can't load on
+   top of blocks that are still being removed. If someone joins while all 8 slots are taken
+   (in practice, only when a player just left and their build is still unloading), they wait
+   and get the next slot that frees up.
+6. **Removing a block** now looks up `blockInfo[block]` and checks
+   `info.ownerId == player.UserId`. Only plot blocks have an entry, so this also replaces the
+   `IsDescendantOf(plotsFolder)` check. The reach check uses the stored cell, which is the same
+   position as before. Placing a block is unchanged, apart from using the template clone.
+
+The tuning constants are at the top of the script: `FRAME_BUDGET`, `MAX_RESERVES_PER_FRAME`,
+`MAX_CREATES_PER_FRAME` and `MAX_DESTROYS_PER_FRAME`. `MAX_CREATES_PER_FRAME` is the main lever
+for client hitching, because it caps how many new parts replicate per frame.
+
+## What players will notice
+
+- **Builds appear over about 1.7 seconds instead of all at once** (20,000 blocks at 200 per frame
+  at 60 Hz). Blocks show up layer by layer from the bottom in save order. If several players join
+  together, the builds load one after another, so the last one can take several seconds to
+  appear. In exchange, nobody's game freezes or rubber-bands.
+- **A leaving player's build disappears over about a third of a second** instead of all at once.
+- **Placing during your own build's load works.** The save's cells are reserved within the first
+  few frames, so you (or anyone else) can't place into a spot the save needs once reservation is
+  done. During those first ~4 frames, a block placed into a not-yet-reserved cell wins, and that
+  saved block is skipped. This is the same rule as before for overlapping cells.
+- **Joining right after someone leaves on a full server** can delay your plot by up to about a
+  third of a second while their build unloads.
+- **Removing your own blocks works as before**, including blocks that are still loading in.
+
+## Things to know for later work
+
+- **Owner and placed time are no longer on the Part.** Server code should read them from
+  `blockInfo` (inside `Builds.server.luau`). If the upcoming placed-time feature needs the value
+  on the client, add it to the part as an attribute (`part:SetAttribute("PlacedAt", ...)`) when
+  the part is created, before parenting. Don't go back to value objects: an attribute adds a few
+  bytes per block, while a value object is a whole extra instance per block.
+- `SavedBuilds.load` is called from the joining player's thread. Once it yields on a DataStore,
+  the code already handles the player leaving before it returns. The test stub currently builds
+  20,000 tables synchronously, which is a small cost (milliseconds, not seconds) and goes away
+  with real saving.
+- Enabling `Workspace.StreamingEnabled`, if the game doesn't already, would further cut what
+  each client has to receive. Clients would then only get blocks near them instead of all
+  8 x 20,000.
+
+## Not verified
+
+These changes pass `selene`, `stylua --check` and `luau-lsp analyze`, but haven't been profiled
+in a live server. Take a new MicroProfiler capture with 8 players, some with full saves, and
+confirm two things: no `Script_Builds` frame goes much past the 3 ms budget, and client
+`ProcessPackets` stays smooth while a build streams in.
